WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.910
SPEAKER: We're trying to solve
one of humanity's biggest

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.570
problems, which is how will
we sustainably generate

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.980
energy in this new world where
energy usage will only go up

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:13.520
from here.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
How do you approach

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.710
the monumental challenge of
innovating renewable energy?

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:30.250
During a 13-year project
that concluded in 2020,

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.490
the Makani team set out to
take on the challenge using

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.520
technology inspired
by kitesurfing.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.580
I'm Priyanka, a developer
advocate with Google Cloud,

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.730
and I wanted to learn more
about how Google Cloud played

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.720
a part in the search for a new
kind of wind-powered energy.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.120
How were the kites
tested and simulated

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.430
to further the
project's progress?

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.560
The team gave me
the inside scoop

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.190
on how Google Cloud powered the
simulations and data processing

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.880
for the energy kites.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.750
I am very excited to find
out how the kite simulation

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.200
logs were collected, processed,
analyzed, and visualized

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.510
to make continuous
improvements to these kites.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.050
But before we jump into
the technical background,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.810
let's see a quick overview of
how wind-powered kites actually

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:15.310
work.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.500
Makani's energy kite
was an aerodynamic wing

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.510
tethered to a ground station.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.850
To begin flight,
the kite used rotors

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.010
to hover vertically in the air.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.110
Once at full tether
length, the kite

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.480
transitioned into
crosswind flight,

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.370
flying in vertical loops.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.830
As the kite flies,
the rotors are

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.580
spun by the wind, generating
electricity that is sent

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.710
down the tether to the grid.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.370
The kite's path is managed
by the flight computer, which

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.920
guides the kite even
in turbulent wins

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.860
and safely returns it
to the ground station.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.310
So those are the basics.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.370
But to understand how the
Makani energy kite worked,

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:02.020
it is helpful to look at how the
mechanics of a traditional wind

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.590
turbine translated
into a crosswind kite.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.590
The blade on a wind
turbine redirect

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.940
wind and creates one force
that pushes on the tower,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.970
and another that pushes
the blade forward.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.240
The forward push of the
blades turns a generator

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.440
to make power.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.800
The crosswind kite is like
freeing a blade from the tower.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.420
The freed blade, now a wing,
is able to grab more wind,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.010
therefore making more power than
it could when stuck to a tower.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.350
The crosswind kite
grabs more wind

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.320
and generates more
power for the same size

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.980
wing from the wind turbine,
but with less materials.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.670
PAULA ECHEVERRI: Trying to
extract power from the wind.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.850
And that's what we would get
closer to when we went testing.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.310
It's like the actual
wind, the physical world

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.830
where our technology
had to work well.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.418
So sometimes the challenges
were it was not windy enough,

00:03:01.418 --> 00:03:03.210
and we were sitting
there waiting for wind.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.895
And then sometimes
it was too windy.

00:03:04.895 --> 00:03:06.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Because flying a real kite

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.100
is expensive, Makani's
team created software

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.160
that would simulate the kite
flying with all the wing

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.470
components, and as
much of the physics

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.320
they could simulate
close to a real kite.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.920
They needed to run the kite at
many different wind directions,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.430
at different altitudes.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.360
So they would generate hundreds
of different configurations

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.310
to simulate the flight.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.260
Then these
configurations were fed

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.570
into Compute Engine
instances that

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.610
would run the kite simulations.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.180
The Compute Engine instances
would be created dynamically

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.000
to run these simulations,
generating log

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.540
files and reports, which would
be then saved in Google Cloud

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.380
Storage.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.060
And these Compute
Engine instances

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.460
would eventually
shut down themselves,

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.310
saving cost and
staying efficient.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.950
After that, the software
engineers in the team

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.500
would pull these logs
and analyze them.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.770
They would look for issues,
red flags, things such as "kite

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.400
did not fly very well."

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.860
There are some issues here.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.720
They would make
new modifications,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.390
and then restart the
simulation process again

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.370
to go through the logs again.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:14.360
And this process keeps repeating
until the team felt confident

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.430
that the real kite
will not crash

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.620
under those circumstances.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.170
Once satisfied with
the simulations,

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.550
they would go to a real testing
site, such as Hawaii or Norway,

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.710
to fly an actual kite.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.860
And as the kite is flying,
it would send the logs

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.360
to the Command Center on ground.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.300
Once the flight
is done, the logs

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.150
are then sent to Google
Cloud Storage for engineers

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:39.440
to analyze again.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.480
They repeat the same
process, look at the data,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.800
analyze it, make modifications
to those configurations,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.990
and then go back
to the first stage.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.370
Run a bunch of
simulations, verify

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.330
that those changes will
make the code fly better

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.270
and won't crash, and will
repeat the entire cycle.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.080
Now, let's talk
about data analysis.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.560
The engineers had two
ways to analyze the data--

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.140
Manual lookup, which
is downloading the file

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:10.220
to their computer, loaded into
MATLAB, or use a Python program

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.440
and run their own
analysis scripts.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.170
But with a project
like this, there

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:18.560
needs to be a well-defined,
consistent analysis that

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.220
could run over and over
on all the flight data.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.130
For that, they built a
data analytics frontend

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:27.290
on App Engine, where
an engineer would

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:31.280
submit a request for all the
logs in a particular time range

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.090
and analyze them with
these predefined criterias.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.510
App Engine will store the
request into Cloud SQL

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.240
and send it to pub/sub
messaging service.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.360
Jenkins master will pull
the analysis request

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.510
and distribute the job to a
bunch of Compute Engine worker

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:48.530
instances.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.720
These instances
were always alive,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.660
waiting for requests
to be sent to them.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.810
The worker would send a
request to Cloud storage

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:00.410
for the log files for that
specific date and time range,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:04.130
process those logs for
analysis, produce the results,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.590
and then store them
back in Cloud SQL

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.150
and publish to App Engine.

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.810
Essentially, the Makani team
ran a bunch of simulations,

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.660
collected a ton of data,
and learned from it.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:18.170
Then they flew a real
kite, studied that data,

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:22.310
and reiterated the process for
the next successful flight.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.430
Google Cloud played a
critical role in this process

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.480
by providing them a scalable
infrastructure for simulations

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.970
and data analysis,
while keeping them

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.450
focused on the real problem
of harnessing wind energy.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:44.420
Though the Makani team's
journey has drawn to an end,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.580
they set up a portfolio
of open source resources,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:52.130
including technical reports,
videos, documentation, patents,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.900
code, and more.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.020
Check out the Makani website
to get a full overview.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.760
If you are interested
in learning more

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.770
about Makani's
journey, they also

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.960
shared a full-length
documentary,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.000
which we have linked below.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.550
Makani's goal was to
harness wind energy,

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.410
and Google Cloud
played a huge role

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.390
in providing the
resources needed

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.780
to do just that without worrying
about the infrastructure,

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.550
scale, and data processing.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.590
In this behind
the scenes series,

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.740
we will continue to explore
how Google Cloud powers

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:24.230
incredible projects,
such as Makani.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:27.290
And I'm looking forward to
taking this journey with you

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:28.840
all.

